hexagon: overlap the HMX weight stream below 33 rows, and fall back when it does not fit

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9 plus the patches before this one in patches/series.

Files:
    ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/matmul-ops.h  the row count at which the HMX 2D path pipelines
    ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/ggml-hexagon.cpp  the serial fallback when the pipelined solve refuses

The problem

htp_mm_hmx_pipeline returned m > 32, thus every row count from 5 to 32 ran the HMX 2D path
with the weight DMA, the dequantization and the multiply in sequence. Measured on the phone on
2026-09-20 with test-backend-ops perf --test-file over the three 4B shapes at Q8_0 with f32
activations (k 2560 n 9216, k 9216 n 2560, k 2560 n 2560), the weight stream runs at 51 to
55 GB/s at 1 to 4 rows, at 41 to 44 GB/s over 5 to 32 rows, and at 51 to 55 GB/s again at
64 rows. At k 2560 n 9216 that is 572 us at 5 rows against 457 us at 64 rows: the op is
slower in wall time for a tenth of the work. The host pads the row count to a multiple of 32
(ne11_padded) and the solver aligns its m chunk down to 32, thus 5 rows and 32 rows do the same
HMX work, and the whole range is one plateau.

Rows 5 to 32 are where the speculative verify runs, where a short prefill lands, and where the
MTP ladder expects its rows to be cheap.

Why 32 was chosen

Not for capacity. The pipelined layout holds a second weight buffer, a second scratch pad and a
second output tile, and its overhead is 7 tiles rather than 5, thus its cost for each column is
twice that of the serial layout. It still fits. A model of htp_mm_hmx_compute_chunks and
htp_mm_hmx_vtcm_layout_build that reproduces the vtcm value of the profile line exactly at
5, 32, 64, 128 and 512 rows gives, at 32 rows and the 8 MB budget:

    shape                     serial m_chunk n_chunk    vtcm  |  pipelined m_chunk n_chunk    vtcm
    k 2560 n 9216 gate_up          32     992  8165376  |          32     480  7907328
    k 9216 n 2560 down             32     256  7981056  |          32     128  7981056
    k 2560 n 2560 proj             32     992  8165376  |          32     480  7907328
    k 2560 n 248320 head           32     992  8165376  |          32     480  7907328

The pipelined layout is the smaller of the two at three of the four shapes, because the solver
spends the budget it saves on a wider n chunk and stops at the same m chunk of 32 either way.
The m chunk stays 32, thus the weight is still streamed once for the whole op, and the
activation stays resident: the layout puts it in group A, which never overlaps the compute
buffers.

Not for correctness either. The pipelined loop guards its prologue on n_chunk_cnt > 1, its
prefetch on i + 2 < n_chunk_cnt and its store on i > 0, and its epilogue drains the last tile,
thus it is correct with one n chunk. Nothing in it reads the row count except
hmx_ceil_div(n_rows, 32), which is 1 for every row count from 1 to 32. The comment on the other
branch of the same if, "Synchronous loop (m <= 32 or fallback)", records the threshold and not a
reason for it.

The change

1. htp_mm_hmx_pipeline returns m > HTP_MM_HMX_MIN_NROWS, which is the row count at which the
   HMX path starts at all, thus every HMX matmul pipelines.

2. ggml_hexagon_precompute_hmx_mm_params tries the serial layout when the solver refuses the
   pipelined one. The doubled cost for each column does exceed the budget for a large reduction
   dimension: at n 4096 and Q8_0 the pipelined layout fits up to k 31680 and the serial layout up
   to k 51040. Without the fallback such a shape would lose the HMX path and fall to HVX, which
   is a regression. No shape of our models is near that limit (the largest k is 9216), thus the
   fallback is a guard and not a path we take.

Prediction, not a measurement

The simulator runs HMX functionally and its timing model never retires an HMX instruction, thus
this change cannot be timed in tools/htp-lab. The expectation is that rows 5 to 32 move from
41 to 44 GB/s to the 51 to 55 GB/s that 1 to 4 rows and 64 rows already reach, because the same
weight stream then overlaps the dequantization. At k 2560 n 9216 that is 572 us to about 460 us,
a factor of about 1.25, and it would make the cost flat from 1 row to 64 rows. The recipe that
measures it is in the report of this work.

