 }
 
+// Whether the HMX 2D path overlaps the weight DMA and the dequantization with the multiply.
+//
+// The threshold was m > 32, which left every row count from 5 to 32 on the serial path. That is
+// not a capacity limit: the pipelined layout needs a second weight buffer, a second scratch pad
+// and a second output tile, and at 32 rows it still fits the 8 MB VTCM with room to spare on
+// every shape of our models (measured on 2026-09-20: k 2560 n 9216 needs 7907328 of 8388608
+// bytes pipelined against 8165376 serial, because the solver spends the budget it saves on a
+// wider n chunk). It is not a correctness limit either: the pipelined loop guards its prologue
+// and its epilogue on n_chunk_cnt and runs correctly with one n chunk.
+//
+// The serial path measured 41 to 44 GB/s of the weight stream over rows 5 to 32 on all three
+// 4B shapes, against 51 to 55 GB/s at 1 to 4 rows and at 64 rows, because the DMA and the
+// multiply do not overlap. The row count at which pipelining starts to pay is thus the row
+// count at which the HMX path starts at all.
+//
+// The caller must fall back to the serial layout when the solver refuses the pipelined one,
+// because a shape with a very large k can exceed the budget with the doubled per-column cost.
 static inline bool htp_mm_hmx_pipeline(uint32_t m) {
-    return m > 32;
+    return m > HTP_MM_HMX_MIN_NROWS;
 }
 
